.TH hsh

.SH NAME
.B hsh
hsh - simple UNIX command interpreter

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hsh
Program is a simple command line interpreter. Executing commans read from standard input. Program mimics behaviour of .B/bin/sh for basic command execution.

.SH BUILD
Developed using C programming language.

.SH INTERACTIVE MODE
Standard output is connected to terminal, by display the prompt:

.PP
$

.PP
The user enters commands, which get parsed & executed. Shell waits for each command to finish before displaying the next prompt. ($)

.SH NON-INTERACTIVE MODE
Input piped/redirected, shell reads commands until EOF & executes each in order.

.PP
echo "la -l" | hsh

.SH BUILTINS
.TP
.B exit [stat]
Exit the shell if numberic stat is provided, shell exits with that stat otherwise, exits with stat 0.

.TP
.B env
Print current environment variables.

.SH EXAMPLES
Interactive mode:

.PP
Interactive mode:

.PP
.nf
$ ls
$ ls -l
$ echo "hello world"
$ exit
.fi

Non-interactive mode:

.PP
.nf
$echo "ls -l" | hsh
.fi

.SH IMPLEMENTATION
Shell uses following system calls and functions:

.PP
fork(), execve(), wait(), stat(), perror(), strtok(), read(), write(), malloc(), free()

.SH DESCRIPTION
Command interpreter that reads input, parses it into tokens and identifies if command is built-in or external. Executes command.

.PP
When shell starts, environment is duplicated so modifications to variables  do not affect parent process. Shell enters a loop that continues until user implements built-in exit command or until EOF is reached.

.PP
Interactive mode - shell displays prompt before reading user input.
Non-interactive mode - input is read direct from standard input without prompting.

.SH EXIT STATUS
Shell exits with the status of last executed command.

.PP
If command is not found, shell exits with status 127.
If user implements the built-in
.B exit
Withoutargument, shell exits using last recorded status.

